00:02:00.474 --> 00:02:03.717
But David, maybe we'll
start with what, you know,

00:02:03.717 --> 00:02:05.221
your "your day job",

00:02:05.221 --> 00:02:09.951
which is co-running Two Sigma.

00:02:09.951 --> 00:02:13.592
Can you explain to
folks what Two Sigma is?

00:02:14.757 --> 00:02:19.312
- Sure. We're an investment
management company.

00:02:19.312 --> 00:02:24.312
We like to say that we're
involved with financial sciences.

00:02:24.799 --> 00:02:27.089
A lot of people say they're
a financial services company.

00:02:27.089 --> 00:02:30.879
We've kind of coined the
expression financial sciences

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:34.486
because we try as best as we can

00:02:34.486 --> 00:02:39.278
to apply the scientific method, data, AI,

00:02:39.278 --> 00:02:42.491
and analytical and quantitative methods

00:02:42.491 --> 00:02:44.709
to the investment process.

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:47.085
And a lot of people think of investing

00:02:47.085 --> 00:02:50.399
as something that is done, you know,

00:02:50.399 --> 00:02:53.037
seat of the pants that, you know,

00:02:53.037 --> 00:02:55.127
you use your intuition gut instincts.

00:02:55.127 --> 00:02:58.209
You know, we've always
believed that, you know,

00:02:58.209 --> 00:02:59.606
applying the science to a problem

00:02:59.606 --> 00:03:01.749
always gives you a better answer.

00:03:01.749 --> 00:03:03.436
It really doesn't matter
what field that you're in.

00:03:03.436 --> 00:03:04.269
You know,

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:05.986
the application of the scientific method

00:03:05.986 --> 00:03:09.440
has really advanced,
virtually every domain

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.349
that we know of, you know,

00:03:12.349 --> 00:03:14.653
when medicine was done,
you know, in the dark ages,

00:03:14.653 --> 00:03:16.406
you know, seat of the pants,

00:03:16.406 --> 00:03:18.849
you know, the outcomes,
weren't all that good.

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:20.696
When the scientific method was adopted

00:03:20.696 --> 00:03:23.691
in the field of medicine,
advanced into something,

00:03:23.691 --> 00:03:25.227
you know, really disciplined.

00:03:25.227 --> 00:03:27.779
Then we began to have the breakthroughs

00:03:27.779 --> 00:03:30.709
that we have in modern medicine today.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:31.542
And, you know,

00:03:31.542 --> 00:03:34.959
I firmly believe that
investing is the same story.

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:39.069
And so Two Sigma, we really,
you know, apply algorithms,

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:42.618
data, various other let's call
them scientific approaches

00:03:42.618 --> 00:03:45.973
to try to drive a better
investment outcome.

00:03:45.973 --> 00:03:48.029
- And it's really
fascinating because you know,

00:03:48.029 --> 00:03:52.099
you have a very deep academic
background and as you know,

00:03:52.099 --> 00:03:53.699
many academics will tell you

00:03:53.699 --> 00:03:55.487
that markets are efficient.

00:03:55.487 --> 00:03:57.189
You can't beat the market, you know,

00:03:57.189 --> 00:03:58.511
just modern portfolio theory,

00:03:58.511 --> 00:04:02.069
just by a big basket of
things, diversify and just,

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:03.713
you know, let it ride so to speak.

00:04:03.713 --> 00:04:06.969
But clearly you and John Overdeck

00:04:06.969 --> 00:04:08.649
who founded Two Sigma with

00:04:08.649 --> 00:04:11.329
believe differently and other
people believe differently.

00:04:11.329 --> 00:04:12.162
(inaudible)

00:04:12.162 --> 00:04:13.669
There is a science to this

00:04:13.669 --> 00:04:15.649
that there actually can be ways to

00:04:15.649 --> 00:04:17.459
outperform the market generally.

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:19.649
What gave you that conviction?

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:21.707
And I am curious on your
entrepreneurial journey,

00:04:21.707 --> 00:04:23.159
how did you fall into this?

00:04:23.159 --> 00:04:24.556
Or how did you make the decision to,

00:04:24.556 --> 00:04:26.852
start an investment firm like Two Sigma?

00:04:27.871 --> 00:04:29.709
- Well, you know,

00:04:29.709 --> 00:04:33.051
the statement about the
markets being efficient is,

00:04:33.051 --> 00:04:35.262
you know, it, you know,

00:04:35.262 --> 00:04:38.338
at a high level it's true.

00:04:38.338 --> 00:04:40.897
You know, the markets are pretty efficient

00:04:40.897 --> 00:04:44.199
and, you know, particularly these days

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:47.629
when the markets, you know,
benefit from, you know,

00:04:47.629 --> 00:04:49.695
ready access to lots of information

00:04:49.695 --> 00:04:53.259
and the markets, you know,

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:55.619
there are many different
ways to access the markets.

00:04:55.619 --> 00:04:56.909
You know, hundreds of years ago,

00:04:56.909 --> 00:04:59.019
the markets were not
particularly efficient

00:04:59.019 --> 00:05:01.417
because, you know, people had
advantages on information.

00:05:01.417 --> 00:05:03.589
People didn't even, you know,

00:05:03.589 --> 00:05:05.191
very few people had access to the markets.

00:05:05.191 --> 00:05:07.609
So, you know, the world
is moving in a direction

00:05:07.609 --> 00:05:10.278
that is making the markets more efficient,

00:05:10.278 --> 00:05:12.558
but they're not perfectly efficient.

00:05:12.558 --> 00:05:14.052
So I think, you know,

00:05:14.052 --> 00:05:15.839
when people do make the statements

00:05:15.839 --> 00:05:17.239
that the markets are efficient,

00:05:17.239 --> 00:05:18.519
I think really what they're saying

00:05:18.519 --> 00:05:20.019
is they're pretty efficient.

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:21.399
So what that means is that

00:05:21.399 --> 00:05:23.529
it's actually a little bit more difficult

00:05:23.529 --> 00:05:24.439
than you might think

00:05:24.439 --> 00:05:26.008
to actually outperform the markets.

00:05:26.008 --> 00:05:27.893
But that doesn't mean it's impossible to,

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:30.939
so the question that you
have is if you have a belief

00:05:30.939 --> 00:05:33.689
that you're going to be
able to outperform an index,

00:05:33.689 --> 00:05:36.619
like the S and P 500, you know,

00:05:36.619 --> 00:05:37.702
what would your,

00:05:39.949 --> 00:05:41.939
some people would call
it your advantage be?

00:05:41.939 --> 00:05:43.959
You know, what, why do
you think, you know,

00:05:43.959 --> 00:05:46.139
what do you have access to,

00:05:46.139 --> 00:05:48.539
that would give you
the ability to do that?

00:05:48.539 --> 00:05:51.679
And in one, you know, our answer would be

00:05:51.679 --> 00:05:53.845
well, we're, gonna be
very scientific about it.

00:05:53.845 --> 00:05:55.780
We're going to use a lot of data.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.466
And, you know, we're
gonna look at the markets,

00:05:58.466 --> 00:06:02.145
primarily using a lot
of computer algorithms

00:06:02.145 --> 00:06:05.139
in a way that it's hard for,

00:06:05.139 --> 00:06:06.989
you know, sort of the old fashioned,

00:06:06.989 --> 00:06:08.849
very gut instinct based approach

00:06:08.849 --> 00:06:09.869
to compete with.

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:10.702
So that would,

00:06:10.702 --> 00:06:12.039
that's the reason that we believe

00:06:12.039 --> 00:06:15.569
that despite the fact that the
markets are pretty efficient,

00:06:15.569 --> 00:06:17.699
that we can do better.

00:06:17.699 --> 00:06:19.779
- And when y'all were starting the firm,

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:21.229
you know, you do,

00:06:21.229 --> 00:06:22.205
you did have,

00:06:22.205 --> 00:06:23.038
(inaudible)

00:06:23.038 --> 00:06:24.089
you did have experience
working at previous

00:06:24.089 --> 00:06:24.922
firms like this.

00:06:24.922 --> 00:06:25.859
Was it like, you know,

00:06:25.859 --> 00:06:27.379
I think we can do better.

00:06:27.379 --> 00:06:28.929
I think we can do better
than the state of the art

00:06:28.929 --> 00:06:29.762
that's out there.

00:06:29.762 --> 00:06:31.302
And I, I'm curious to, how did you

00:06:31.302 --> 00:06:34.079
and John have some initial algorithms

00:06:34.079 --> 00:06:35.709
that you had back tested and, you know,

00:06:35.709 --> 00:06:36.966
for those who don't know backtesting is,

00:06:36.966 --> 00:06:38.999
hey, we have a theory,
we have an algorithm,

00:06:38.999 --> 00:06:40.819
let's take this algorithm on historic data

00:06:40.819 --> 00:06:42.019
and see how it would have performed.

00:06:42.019 --> 00:06:42.852
And you're like, yeah,

00:06:42.852 --> 00:06:44.159
I think we got something interesting here.

00:06:44.159 --> 00:06:46.019
How did you, how did it start?

00:06:46.019 --> 00:06:49.162
I'm always curious about that
phase of an organization.

00:06:49.162 --> 00:06:51.476
- No. It wasn't like
that. It was more just a,

00:06:51.476 --> 00:06:56.476
you know, it's like any
entrepreneurial exercise.

00:06:57.009 --> 00:06:59.889
I think that people just have
a belief and a conviction.

00:06:59.889 --> 00:07:03.639
And so you just, you know,

00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:05.809
we started with a blank sheet of paper,

00:07:05.809 --> 00:07:07.699
but just, you know, literally a belief

00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:10.060
that this is the future and, you know,

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:12.915
no different than I think
virtually all tech startups,

00:07:12.915 --> 00:07:15.407
most of the successful companies

00:07:15.407 --> 00:07:16.893
that you have today

00:07:16.893 --> 00:07:19.079
that have emerged that of Silicon valley

00:07:19.079 --> 00:07:20.469
and elsewhere, you know,

00:07:20.469 --> 00:07:22.433
they've often just started with a belief

00:07:22.433 --> 00:07:26.099
that this is how, you
know, we can do better.

00:07:26.099 --> 00:07:29.882
And, you know, so really just a belief.

00:07:30.829 --> 00:07:34.030
- Yeah. And that belief
has been validated.

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:36.549
I am curious, I mean, as someone who has,

00:07:36.549 --> 00:07:39.169
who studies markets
with a scientific lens,

00:07:39.169 --> 00:07:41.179
as you mentioned, you know, what,

00:07:41.179 --> 00:07:43.851
there's been a lot of talk about

00:07:43.851 --> 00:07:45.799
a case shape recovery about

00:07:45.799 --> 00:07:47.169
some people are doing just fine.

00:07:47.169 --> 00:07:48.369
Other people are doing less fine,

00:07:48.369 --> 00:07:49.458
even before the pandemic,

00:07:49.458 --> 00:07:50.727
people have been talking about

00:07:50.727 --> 00:07:53.078
how automation and technology

00:07:53.078 --> 00:07:54.659
they're going to benefit some folks,

00:07:54.659 --> 00:07:56.429
but they could hurt other other folks.

00:07:56.429 --> 00:07:59.099
I know this is an area that
you've thought deeply about,

00:07:59.099 --> 00:08:00.590
and obviously you're in it

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:02.017
and in certain ways.

00:08:02.017 --> 00:08:04.769
Where do you think we're heading

00:08:04.769 --> 00:08:05.989
from an economic point of view?

00:08:05.989 --> 00:08:07.682
What do you think the
impacts of the pandemic are?

00:08:07.682 --> 00:08:10.029
What do you think is going
on in markets right now?

00:08:10.029 --> 00:08:11.449
And what do you think we
need to be hopeful for,

00:08:11.449 --> 00:08:14.089
or watch out for a little bit longer term?

00:08:14.089 --> 00:08:14.922
- Well, you know,

00:08:14.922 --> 00:08:16.563
these are really gonna be
just my personal views,

00:08:16.563 --> 00:08:18.567
not of the company.

00:08:18.567 --> 00:08:21.419
You know, I just wanna be clear.

00:08:21.419 --> 00:08:23.529
This, isn't the kind of
thing that the company

00:08:23.529 --> 00:08:27.719
that these are not exactly
the kind of investment

00:08:27.719 --> 00:08:29.339
approaches the firm takes.

00:08:29.339 --> 00:08:32.251
So speaking really just
entirely personally,

00:08:32.251 --> 00:08:34.273
and you know, this is,

00:08:34.273 --> 00:08:39.273
I don't think actually
a new trend in America.

00:08:40.089 --> 00:08:41.889
It really does appear that

00:08:41.889 --> 00:08:45.766
our economy has been moving in a direction

00:08:45.766 --> 00:08:49.619
where certain people are doing, you know,

00:08:49.619 --> 00:08:50.779
better and better,

00:08:50.779 --> 00:08:54.379
and other people are falling behind.

00:08:54.379 --> 00:08:55.309
And, you know,

00:08:55.309 --> 00:08:57.109
I think that this accelerated

00:08:57.109 --> 00:08:58.493
during the pandemic

00:08:58.493 --> 00:09:01.589
for a number of reasons,

00:09:01.589 --> 00:09:03.709
I can really quickly go into them.

00:09:03.709 --> 00:09:05.829
I mean, we could talk about
this for hours and hours,

00:09:05.829 --> 00:09:09.429
but I think a lot of the bifurcation

00:09:09.429 --> 00:09:12.719
of economic outcomes are being caused by

00:09:12.719 --> 00:09:14.599
fundamental changes in our economy

00:09:14.599 --> 00:09:18.029
that are driven by technology in,

00:09:18.029 --> 00:09:19.999
you know, the rapid transformation

00:09:19.999 --> 00:09:21.776
of the global economy, you know,

00:09:21.776 --> 00:09:24.978
because of technological innovation,

00:09:24.978 --> 00:09:27.801
the internet, you know,

00:09:27.801 --> 00:09:32.544
you're all familiar with,
you know, obviously Google

00:09:32.544 --> 00:09:33.959
and Uber and, you know,

00:09:33.959 --> 00:09:37.159
there are dozens of
companies that, you know,

00:09:37.159 --> 00:09:39.721
are virtually brand new
and, you know, Amazon,

00:09:39.721 --> 00:09:42.242
these firms have changed
the way all aspects

00:09:42.242 --> 00:09:44.269
of our economy function.

00:09:44.269 --> 00:09:48.799
And when you have a big
economic dislocation occurring,

00:09:48.799 --> 00:09:50.409
driven by technological change,

00:09:50.409 --> 00:09:52.889
that is going to, you know,

00:09:52.889 --> 00:09:54.039
restack the deck,

00:09:54.039 --> 00:09:56.219
the people that benefit will be different

00:09:56.219 --> 00:09:57.731
than the people that benefited before.

00:09:57.731 --> 00:10:01.519
Other people who thought
that they had stability

00:10:01.519 --> 00:10:04.399
in their work will find that
they no longer have stability,

00:10:04.399 --> 00:10:08.009
the kind of skills that you
need to get ahead, change.

00:10:08.009 --> 00:10:08.842
You know,

00:10:08.842 --> 00:10:11.449
everyone knows today that technical skills

00:10:11.449 --> 00:10:14.360
are more valuable than
they were ever before.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:17.977
People that have mastered
certain kinds of technologies,

00:10:17.977 --> 00:10:21.893
you know AI is one of them have, you know,

00:10:21.893 --> 00:10:24.987
had unlimited opportunity
in the new economy.

00:10:24.987 --> 00:10:27.409
People with, you know,

00:10:27.409 --> 00:10:28.850
certain other kinds of skill sets

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:30.989
where automation has taken over,

00:10:30.989 --> 00:10:33.290
have been falling behind.

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:35.549
And this trend accelerated

00:10:35.549 --> 00:10:37.199
during the pandemic

00:10:37.199 --> 00:10:40.374
in part, because people were investing,

00:10:40.374 --> 00:10:44.094
firms were investing very
heavily in more automation,

00:10:44.094 --> 00:10:47.769
looking at more ways to reduce dependence

00:10:47.769 --> 00:10:49.819
on traditional human based approaches

00:10:49.819 --> 00:10:50.869
for doing things.

00:10:50.869 --> 00:10:51.702
So, you know,

00:10:51.702 --> 00:10:53.649
this is one of the many
factors that are driving,

00:10:53.649 --> 00:10:58.649
you know what I would call, an alarming

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.359
change in our economy,

00:11:02.359 --> 00:11:04.619
which, you know, we have to,

00:11:04.619 --> 00:11:08.059
as a society we need to address.

00:11:08.059 --> 00:11:10.159
- And do you think this is
something different about

00:11:10.159 --> 00:11:11.706
what we're going through now versus

00:11:11.706 --> 00:11:14.229
previous periods of technological change?

00:11:14.229 --> 00:11:16.069
Obviously, people talk about
the industrial revolution.

00:11:16.069 --> 00:11:18.999
Yes, people who are
driving a horse carriages,

00:11:18.999 --> 00:11:20.539
or I guess even the horses themselves,

00:11:20.539 --> 00:11:22.099
I guess horses probably
did lose a lot of work

00:11:22.099 --> 00:11:22.948
during the

00:11:22.948 --> 00:11:23.781
(inaudible)

00:11:23.781 --> 00:11:25.239
the automation with cars,

00:11:25.239 --> 00:11:27.159
but a lot of people point to that and say,

00:11:27.159 --> 00:11:29.599
well, you know, yes, a lot
of there was a lot of flux,

00:11:29.599 --> 00:11:30.579
a lot of change and things,

00:11:30.579 --> 00:11:34.216
but at the same time,
overall jobs were created.

00:11:34.216 --> 00:11:36.229
There was a broad based middle-class

00:11:36.229 --> 00:11:37.641
that came out of the
industrial revolution.

00:11:37.641 --> 00:11:40.592
So this revolution might
not be any different.

00:11:40.592 --> 00:11:44.029
It could be change, but
overall it will be better.

00:11:44.029 --> 00:11:45.319
But then you hear on the other side,

00:11:45.319 --> 00:11:47.268
people say, well, I don't know,

00:11:47.268 --> 00:11:49.199
artificial intelligence, automation,

00:11:49.199 --> 00:11:51.069
they're kind of getting
deeper into what human beings

00:11:51.069 --> 00:11:52.680
are oftentimes capable of.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:55.412
Where do you fall on that debate?

00:11:56.489 --> 00:12:00.754
- Well, again, a very
complicated question,

00:12:00.754 --> 00:12:05.754
try to give over probably
over simple simplified answer,

00:12:07.109 --> 00:12:09.819
but I do not believe

00:12:09.819 --> 00:12:12.604
that there will be a lack of jobs.

00:12:12.604 --> 00:12:17.469
There is plenty of work
that only humans can do.

00:12:17.469 --> 00:12:21.587
And as we automate certain kinds of work

00:12:21.587 --> 00:12:25.399
we're freeing up people
to do other kinds of work,

00:12:25.399 --> 00:12:28.836
and, you know, we have an aging population

00:12:28.836 --> 00:12:30.359
there's gonna be a need for

00:12:30.359 --> 00:12:32.013
more people, just as one example,

00:12:32.013 --> 00:12:35.367
more people to take care of the elderly.

00:12:35.367 --> 00:12:38.489
There is a crumbling infrastructure

00:12:38.489 --> 00:12:39.719
all around America.

00:12:39.719 --> 00:12:42.019
We need more people to rebuild this.

00:12:42.019 --> 00:12:43.628
You know, the robots are not gonna be

00:12:43.628 --> 00:12:45.293
handling this kind of work.

00:12:45.293 --> 00:12:48.629
People have now changed
their shopping habits.

00:12:48.629 --> 00:12:51.419
We need more people to
deliver all the packages.

00:12:51.419 --> 00:12:54.144
You know, it's unlikely
that we're gonna have,

00:12:54.144 --> 00:12:55.669
you know, despite all the hype,

00:12:55.669 --> 00:12:58.759
I don't think drones are
gonna be really changing

00:12:58.759 --> 00:13:02.759
how we get deliveries in,
you know, anytime soon.

00:13:02.759 --> 00:13:04.629
So the amount of work for people to do,

00:13:04.629 --> 00:13:05.996
it's not going away.

00:13:05.996 --> 00:13:08.738
But the problem is that,

00:13:08.738 --> 00:13:11.159
you know, we have to
have an adjustment here

00:13:11.159 --> 00:13:12.533
and we have to decide if,

00:13:12.533 --> 00:13:16.485
we're going to be able
to pay middle-class wages

00:13:16.485 --> 00:13:18.625
for the new kind of work.

00:13:18.625 --> 00:13:20.359
And, you know,

00:13:20.359 --> 00:13:22.822
it is possible that, you know,

00:13:22.822 --> 00:13:24.075
a lot of the new work,

00:13:24.075 --> 00:13:27.454
people are only gonna want to
pay relatively low wages for,

00:13:27.454 --> 00:13:31.346
and maybe these workers
won't have the ability to,

00:13:31.346 --> 00:13:33.993
in any way organized to get higher wages.

00:13:33.993 --> 00:13:36.587
So you might end up with a relatively

00:13:36.587 --> 00:13:39.769
large amount of work

00:13:39.769 --> 00:13:42.569
at wages that are not really suitable

00:13:42.569 --> 00:13:44.431
for middle-class living.

00:13:44.431 --> 00:13:46.799
Now, it, you know,

00:13:46.799 --> 00:13:48.588
the previous industrial revolution,

00:13:48.588 --> 00:13:50.609
which is, has been well studied,

00:13:50.609 --> 00:13:51.894
of course the outcome was

00:13:51.894 --> 00:13:55.173
a vibrant thriving middle class.

00:13:55.173 --> 00:13:57.118
But, you know, you have to remember

00:13:57.118 --> 00:14:01.299
that the transition from say the beginning

00:14:01.299 --> 00:14:02.681
of the industrial revolution

00:14:02.681 --> 00:14:05.522
to the thriving middle-class,

00:14:05.522 --> 00:14:08.379
you know, also brought with it, you know,

00:14:08.379 --> 00:14:10.030
World War one, World War two,

00:14:10.030 --> 00:14:12.189
there was a lot of trouble,

00:14:12.189 --> 00:14:13.879
there was a depression in there.

00:14:13.879 --> 00:14:16.631
So it wasn't really a smooth transition,

00:14:16.631 --> 00:14:18.472
but eventually you got to a spot

00:14:18.472 --> 00:14:21.219
that was actually pretty good.

00:14:21.219 --> 00:14:22.639
The goal here is to make sure that

00:14:22.639 --> 00:14:24.325
we don't have a bumpy ride.

00:14:24.325 --> 00:14:26.788
Certainly we don't want to
repeat anything like that

00:14:26.788 --> 00:14:31.189
when we go to this new economic phase

00:14:31.189 --> 00:14:34.862
that is being heavily driven
by a new wave of technology.

00:14:35.814 --> 00:14:36.647
- Yeah.

00:14:36.647 --> 00:14:37.749
No, it's a really good point.

00:14:37.749 --> 00:14:39.719
And, you know, oftentimes the economics

00:14:39.719 --> 00:14:42.119
and the history of things

00:14:42.119 --> 00:14:44.919
like wars and often comes get disjointed,

00:14:44.919 --> 00:14:46.669
but they are connected
in probably more ways

00:14:46.669 --> 00:14:50.819
than even the history books,
try to draw connections.

00:14:50.819 --> 00:14:52.159
I mean, what do you think, you know,

00:14:52.159 --> 00:14:54.379
just stay on this question,

00:14:54.379 --> 00:14:55.889
and I'm getting a lot of questions from,

00:14:55.889 --> 00:14:57.649
I'm assuming young people on, you know,

00:14:57.649 --> 00:14:59.309
what advice would you
have for them, you know,

00:14:59.309 --> 00:15:01.999
given the world that we're
going into and all that.

00:15:01.999 --> 00:15:03.294
So I'll, it'll be a double question.

00:15:03.294 --> 00:15:05.460
What advice do you have
for them as individuals,

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:06.805
but also what advice do you,

00:15:06.805 --> 00:15:08.369
are you thinking about for society?

00:15:08.369 --> 00:15:09.279
I know you've thought,

00:15:09.279 --> 00:15:10.469
and you've written about some of this,

00:15:10.469 --> 00:15:11.709
you know, what's the role of government,

00:15:11.709 --> 00:15:13.925
the not-for-profit sector,
the for-profit sector.

00:15:13.925 --> 00:15:15.632
How do you think about this?

00:15:16.729 --> 00:15:21.722
- Well, you know, government,

00:15:23.789 --> 00:15:26.019
again, Sal heavy question,

00:15:26.019 --> 00:15:27.519
give me an easy question.

00:15:27.519 --> 00:15:28.739
The, you know,

00:15:28.739 --> 00:15:32.449
the role of government is
obviously incredibly important

00:15:32.449 --> 00:15:33.639
in society.

00:15:33.639 --> 00:15:36.193
You know, I think people in many,

00:15:36.193 --> 00:15:39.583
in my own personal view
is people essentially take

00:15:39.583 --> 00:15:43.389
government for granted because

00:15:43.389 --> 00:15:45.549
in many cases, what
the government is doing

00:15:45.549 --> 00:15:47.711
is providing infrastructure.

00:15:47.711 --> 00:15:49.175
They're providing things

00:15:49.175 --> 00:15:51.419
that are kind of behind the scenes,

00:15:51.419 --> 00:15:52.920
and then all the excitement

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:54.399
that the infrastructure

00:15:54.399 --> 00:15:58.279
that government provides then
leads to the vibrant economy

00:15:58.279 --> 00:15:59.469
and the good outcomes.

00:15:59.469 --> 00:16:00.302
So the, you know,

00:16:00.302 --> 00:16:02.331
the private sector is
built upon the foundation

00:16:02.331 --> 00:16:04.473
that the government has created.

00:16:04.473 --> 00:16:07.079
And, you know, when I'm
talking about infrastructure,

00:16:07.079 --> 00:16:08.709
I'm using a very broad definition.

00:16:08.709 --> 00:16:10.309
You know, the government, you know,

00:16:10.309 --> 00:16:13.429
in many cases is involved
with physical infrastructure,

00:16:13.429 --> 00:16:15.374
like roads and bridges and tunnels.

00:16:15.374 --> 00:16:18.799
And the government may very
well be involved with more

00:16:18.799 --> 00:16:21.293
modern infrastructure as well.

00:16:21.293 --> 00:16:22.699
You know, either directly

00:16:22.699 --> 00:16:24.547
or indirectly for policy decisions.

00:16:24.547 --> 00:16:27.007
The government is obviously
involved with education.

00:16:27.007 --> 00:16:30.559
The government is involved
with the rule of law,

00:16:30.559 --> 00:16:33.334
creating an environment
where businesses can thrive.

00:16:33.334 --> 00:16:36.091
The you know, there is
no such thing by the way,

00:16:36.091 --> 00:16:40.189
as a capitalism, without
a government intervention,

00:16:40.189 --> 00:16:42.029
you know, the economy is...

00:16:42.029 --> 00:16:42.999
All economies,

00:16:42.999 --> 00:16:45.318
the US and every country in the world

00:16:45.318 --> 00:16:48.429
they're, you know, they're
not just, you know,

00:16:48.429 --> 00:16:50.209
the private sector without any rules,

00:16:50.209 --> 00:16:52.259
the government creates rules of the road.

00:16:52.259 --> 00:16:54.340
And so this infrastructure very broadly

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:57.245
is critical for a thriving,

00:16:57.245 --> 00:16:59.559
not only the economy,

00:16:59.559 --> 00:17:02.099
but just for people to live good lives.

00:17:02.099 --> 00:17:03.859
And so I think that, you know,

00:17:03.859 --> 00:17:05.709
government well-functioning government

00:17:05.709 --> 00:17:08.181
is incredibly important.

00:17:08.181 --> 00:17:11.519
- And what's your advice?

00:17:11.519 --> 00:17:12.352
I mean,

00:17:12.352 --> 00:17:13.399
there's a lot that we could talk for hours

00:17:13.399 --> 00:17:16.059
just on the governance
piece or for profit.

00:17:16.059 --> 00:17:17.849
And we'll talk about
philanthropy in a second,

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:19.409
but on the question of
individuals, you know,

00:17:19.409 --> 00:17:20.546
for a lot of the young people
who are asking questions,

00:17:20.546 --> 00:17:22.769
like, what should I do with my life people

00:17:22.769 --> 00:17:23.602
(inaudible)

00:17:23.602 --> 00:17:24.435
should I go into computer science?

00:17:24.435 --> 00:17:25.268
How should I do it?

00:17:25.268 --> 00:17:26.101
How should I navigate?

00:17:26.101 --> 00:17:27.819
You know, what, what's
your message for folks,

00:17:27.819 --> 00:17:30.874
how they be thinking about
their life, given what is,

00:17:30.874 --> 00:17:32.499
what seems like likely to happen

00:17:32.499 --> 00:17:34.046
the cross-currents of technology

00:17:34.046 --> 00:17:35.662
and the economy and all of that?

00:17:36.695 --> 00:17:40.069
- Well, you know, first and foremost,

00:17:40.069 --> 00:17:43.089
I would start by telling
people that, you know,

00:17:43.089 --> 00:17:46.493
they really need to pursue their passions.

00:17:46.493 --> 00:17:48.207
There's no right answer.

00:17:48.207 --> 00:17:50.469
Like if I were to say yeah,

00:17:50.469 --> 00:17:52.146
the right thing to do is, you know,

00:17:52.146 --> 00:17:54.398
become a, you know, AI specialist,

00:17:54.398 --> 00:17:56.436
you know, sure that,

00:17:56.436 --> 00:17:58.679
you know, there's gonna
be a lot of opportunity

00:17:58.679 --> 00:17:59.799
for such people,

00:17:59.799 --> 00:18:01.959
but that is not going to necessarily

00:18:01.959 --> 00:18:04.649
be interesting to everyone.

00:18:04.649 --> 00:18:07.166
So I think the key thing is that,

00:18:07.166 --> 00:18:10.299
you know, in today's world, you know,

00:18:10.299 --> 00:18:14.251
look it is, you know, it's
a relatively tough world.

00:18:14.251 --> 00:18:16.329
You know, people aren't struggling

00:18:16.329 --> 00:18:18.319
to find their way in our economy

00:18:18.319 --> 00:18:19.789
because it's changing very quickly

00:18:19.789 --> 00:18:21.435
and the skills that people
need are not obvious.

00:18:21.435 --> 00:18:22.839
But I think, you know,

00:18:22.839 --> 00:18:23.846
the thing that's most important

00:18:23.846 --> 00:18:26.833
is, you know, to have the
passion for what you're doing.

00:18:26.833 --> 00:18:29.696
And if it's something
that you really wanna do,

00:18:29.696 --> 00:18:32.629
it's much more likely that you will,

00:18:32.629 --> 00:18:36.297
you know, put the energy
into it, to succeed.

00:18:36.297 --> 00:18:41.297
I, you know, look, the
obvious answer of course,

00:18:41.839 --> 00:18:43.836
is that STEM skills are more important.

00:18:43.836 --> 00:18:46.662
You know, you should really get
your basic math skills down.

00:18:46.662 --> 00:18:48.999
You know, you should spend
time on the Khan Academy

00:18:48.999 --> 00:18:51.119
to perfect, you know, the material

00:18:51.119 --> 00:18:54.352
that wonderful organization
has put together.

00:18:54.352 --> 00:18:57.211
But I would definitely vote for

00:18:57.211 --> 00:18:59.649
you gotta be pursuing something

00:18:59.649 --> 00:19:02.204
that you're, that brings you joy.

00:19:02.204 --> 00:19:04.279
Your life will be better.

00:19:04.279 --> 00:19:05.112
You know,

00:19:05.112 --> 00:19:07.940
don't pursue something simply
because you read somewhere

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:10.649
that it's gonna be the
carer of the future.

00:19:10.649 --> 00:19:12.243
And it's very hard to predict the future.

00:19:12.243 --> 00:19:13.493
So you, you know,

00:19:13.493 --> 00:19:15.766
you might end up getting
that totally wrong.

00:19:15.766 --> 00:19:19.909
Another thing I would
mention is that, you know,

00:19:19.909 --> 00:19:21.325
the world is changing very quickly

00:19:21.325 --> 00:19:23.324
and what this means is that

00:19:23.324 --> 00:19:25.210
you're gonna have to really become,

00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:28.395
you know, a constant lifelong learner.

00:19:28.395 --> 00:19:31.246
And so whatever you know
your interests are today,

00:19:31.246 --> 00:19:34.059
whatever the skills that you developed

00:19:34.059 --> 00:19:35.961
through your education today,

00:19:35.961 --> 00:19:38.700
keep in mind that you're
going to have to continue

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:41.476
to invest to make sure that,

00:19:41.476 --> 00:19:44.739
you know, you're ready
for the next curve ball

00:19:44.739 --> 00:19:47.289
that our vibrant world
is gonna throw at you.

00:19:47.289 --> 00:19:50.689
So this, again, points to
trying to go in a direction

00:19:50.689 --> 00:19:51.522
where you have a passion

00:19:51.522 --> 00:19:55.565
it's kind of hard to force
yourself to continue to improve

00:19:55.565 --> 00:20:00.099
and gain new skills in something
that you don't like at all.

00:20:00.099 --> 00:20:02.103
So passion, passion, passion.

00:20:02.103 --> 00:20:04.009
- I couldn't agree with that more.

00:20:04.009 --> 00:20:04.842
And it's funny cause you know,

00:20:04.842 --> 00:20:07.435
I remember when I was a kid and I had,

00:20:07.435 --> 00:20:09.619
I did have a passion
for math and for science

00:20:09.619 --> 00:20:11.479
and for things like
that and for computers.

00:20:11.479 --> 00:20:13.566
But I also had a passion
for art and drawing and,

00:20:13.566 --> 00:20:16.129
you know, the messages
that I got, oftentimes as,

00:20:16.129 --> 00:20:17.679
you know, first-generation
immigrant family,

00:20:17.679 --> 00:20:20.039
like that's not gonna be practical Sal,

00:20:20.039 --> 00:20:21.169
that's a nice hobby,

00:20:21.169 --> 00:20:23.169
but you know, you fast forward
today and you see that,

00:20:23.169 --> 00:20:26.817
wow, you know, fields like
design are actually highly after

00:20:26.817 --> 00:20:30.089
and can be very pragmatic

00:20:30.089 --> 00:20:31.731
and you can do very well in them.

00:20:31.731 --> 00:20:33.443
And that even, you know,

00:20:33.443 --> 00:20:37.656
my own journey, you
know, I used to be in a,

00:20:37.656 --> 00:20:39.549
at least a tangential field to yours,

00:20:39.549 --> 00:20:41.429
but we were a different
type of investment firm,

00:20:41.429 --> 00:20:42.426
but we were in markets and things,

00:20:42.426 --> 00:20:44.286
and I enjoyed that and
I had a passion for it,

00:20:44.286 --> 00:20:46.776
but I had this even deeper
passion for education.

00:20:46.776 --> 00:20:49.019
And that if you just create
a little bit of space for it,

00:20:49.019 --> 00:20:52.249
you know, we're in a brave
new world where opportunity

00:20:52.249 --> 00:20:54.279
that no one could have
predicted the job that I have

00:20:54.279 --> 00:20:57.269
or, even the type of organization.

00:20:57.269 --> 00:20:58.677
So I think that's really
good advice is that

00:20:58.677 --> 00:21:00.837
the combination of having
really strong skills,

00:21:00.837 --> 00:21:02.819
probably anything that you wanna do

00:21:02.819 --> 00:21:04.659
at some form of reasonable scale

00:21:04.659 --> 00:21:06.553
and impact will involve
some type of technology.

00:21:06.553 --> 00:21:09.091
But then you couple
that with your passions

00:21:09.091 --> 00:21:12.763
and you can be quite formidable.

00:21:12.763 --> 00:21:16.090
What you know, moving on to the,

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:19.489
another big piece of your
life, which is philanthropy.

00:21:19.489 --> 00:21:20.322
And as I mentioned,

00:21:20.322 --> 00:21:23.404
I'm very biased because actually
both you and John have been

00:21:23.404 --> 00:21:25.459
great supporters of Khan,

00:21:25.459 --> 00:21:27.479
your families have been great
supporters of Khan Academy

00:21:27.479 --> 00:21:28.312
over the years.

00:21:28.312 --> 00:21:30.139
But what got you into philanthropy?

00:21:30.139 --> 00:21:32.199
Arguably at a relatively young age,

00:21:32.199 --> 00:21:33.569
a lot of people go through

00:21:33.569 --> 00:21:36.769
their stage where they're kind of earning,

00:21:36.769 --> 00:21:37.779
and then later in life,

00:21:37.779 --> 00:21:39.126
they start going into philanthropy.

00:21:39.126 --> 00:21:40.479
What got you into it?

00:21:40.479 --> 00:21:41.699
Why did you prioritize it?

00:21:41.699 --> 00:21:44.229
And how do you, what's your
framework of philanthropy?

00:21:44.229 --> 00:21:48.059
Where do you think that you can
make the biggest difference?

00:21:48.059 --> 00:21:53.059
- Well, you know, the journey

00:21:54.023 --> 00:21:58.159
that I took into philanthropy
started a long time ago.

00:21:58.159 --> 00:22:03.159
You know, doing things to help people has

00:22:05.348 --> 00:22:09.419
always, you know, it, you
know, we all have to do that.

00:22:09.419 --> 00:22:10.252
It, you know,

00:22:10.252 --> 00:22:14.438
I think that everyone in
society has some obligation.

00:22:14.438 --> 00:22:17.119
I mean, some people say
you should, you know,

00:22:17.119 --> 00:22:19.529
if everyone gives back a little bit more

00:22:19.529 --> 00:22:22.209
than they take the world would be,

00:22:22.209 --> 00:22:24.149
you know, forever a better place.

00:22:24.149 --> 00:22:26.762
And so if you think just logically,

00:22:27.755 --> 00:22:29.415
you know,

00:22:29.415 --> 00:22:31.569
how did you get to where you are?

00:22:31.569 --> 00:22:33.189
How did I get to where I am?

00:22:33.189 --> 00:22:34.105
I was, you know,

00:22:34.105 --> 00:22:36.291
benefiting from essentially

00:22:36.291 --> 00:22:40.852
all kinds of philanthropic things

00:22:40.852 --> 00:22:42.121
over my lifetime.

00:22:42.121 --> 00:22:45.149
Education in America, you know,

00:22:45.149 --> 00:22:47.949
is largely supported with philanthropy.

00:22:47.949 --> 00:22:49.794
I got a really good education.

00:22:49.794 --> 00:22:51.476
Everywhere i look,

00:22:51.476 --> 00:22:54.921
I'm the beneficiary of other people

00:22:54.921 --> 00:22:59.129
who decided that they wanted
to give back to society.

00:22:59.129 --> 00:23:01.451
And that made me recognize

00:23:01.451 --> 00:23:03.176
at a relatively early age that,

00:23:03.176 --> 00:23:05.789
you know, I wanna do the same thing.

00:23:05.789 --> 00:23:06.999
And also, you know,

00:23:06.999 --> 00:23:08.909
as you start to do philanthropic things,

00:23:08.909 --> 00:23:11.679
it makes you actually want to do more.

00:23:11.679 --> 00:23:14.439
You know, so I find
philanthropy to be, you know,

00:23:14.439 --> 00:23:15.479
in a way addictive.

00:23:15.479 --> 00:23:17.385
You know, you get your feet wet,

00:23:17.385 --> 00:23:19.892
you get involved with an
organization, you help them.

00:23:19.892 --> 00:23:22.389
You know, you give with your heart,

00:23:22.389 --> 00:23:24.082
you give a little bit from your checkbook,

00:23:24.082 --> 00:23:26.039
whatever you can do.

00:23:26.039 --> 00:23:28.782
And then I think virtually
everyone concludes, wow,

00:23:28.782 --> 00:23:30.759
that felt great.

00:23:30.759 --> 00:23:32.679
And you know,

00:23:32.679 --> 00:23:35.229
it's so easy to get
involved with philanthropy

00:23:35.229 --> 00:23:37.249
because there are needs everywhere.

00:23:37.249 --> 00:23:38.389
And so, you know,

00:23:38.389 --> 00:23:40.349
I started in a small way

00:23:40.349 --> 00:23:43.709
and my own journey was, well, you know,

00:23:43.709 --> 00:23:46.860
how can I apply what I know?

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:47.949
You know,

00:23:47.949 --> 00:23:49.902
what are the special things

00:23:49.902 --> 00:23:52.649
that I might be able to do to help?

00:23:52.649 --> 00:23:54.509
And, you know,

00:23:54.509 --> 00:23:56.449
one of the area that
I ended up focusing on

00:23:56.449 --> 00:24:00.611
and that my, the Siegel
family endowment focuses on

00:24:00.611 --> 00:24:03.701
is essentially to help people,

00:24:03.701 --> 00:24:07.359
in the journey through tech,

00:24:07.359 --> 00:24:09.989
the technological change that
our society is going through,

00:24:09.989 --> 00:24:13.489
how are we going to prepare
and help people navigate

00:24:13.489 --> 00:24:15.891
an increasingly technical

00:24:15.891 --> 00:24:18.799
and technologically driven society?

00:24:18.799 --> 00:24:21.309
And these changes that
are occurring, you know,

00:24:21.309 --> 00:24:24.489
as we talked about actually
earlier in this call Sal,

00:24:24.489 --> 00:24:27.839
you know, are very, very challenging

00:24:27.839 --> 00:24:31.243
for individuals, for
institutions, for governments.

00:24:31.243 --> 00:24:35.359
This technological
transformation is something that,

00:24:35.359 --> 00:24:37.479
you know, I think that, you know,

00:24:37.479 --> 00:24:39.901
my foundation can help you know,

00:24:39.901 --> 00:24:42.303
improve the outcomes a little bit.

00:24:42.303 --> 00:24:46.362
And that's what we're really
committed to be focused on.

00:24:47.659 --> 00:24:50.039
- And I'm gonna go, a ton of
questions as a question related

00:24:50.039 --> 00:24:51.062
